-- Introduction
INSERT INTO public.reading_sections (content_id, sort_order, heading, body, quote) VALUES
('c2b3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f12345678901', 0, 'Introduction',
'Hello Ladyboss! I''m Razieh Mirzaei, the author of Courageous Character. If you''re worried that embracing a Courageous Character might make you lose your loved ones, let me put that fear to rest.

These days you hear things like "The winner ends up alone" or "If you grow, those around you will be left behind." But all of these are lies. People say these things to frighten you and stop you from progressing.

What we''ve seen is exactly the opposite. Out of every 10 people who experienced personal growth, 9 of them inspired change in those around them. Trust that when you take steps forward, your loved ones will also wake up and join you.

The single most important point is: until your Courageous Character is fully developed, you don''t need to broadcast what you''re learning. Talk is just hot air. A Ladyboss shows results. You can only influence others through your actions and outcomes, not with words. First, in complete silence, read to the end and do the exercises. Very soon, the people around you will come to you and ask, "What have you been doing?"',
'When you become a better version of yourself, those around you will start moving forward too.');

-- Chapter 1
INSERT INTO public.reading_sections (content_id, sort_order, heading, body, quote) VALUES
('c2b3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f12345678901', 1, 'The Deep Roots of Timidity',
'If you had all the courage in the world right now, what would you do? Take 30 seconds and write down your answer.

I want to congratulate you—first, because you already know that shyness has kept you from things you wanted to achieve. Second, because you know that shyness is not innate and can be overcome with learning and practice.

I was a girl from the traditional culture of the south of Iran. I realized I needed to become independent. I came to Tehran, quit my engineering job, and started in sales with no salary, no support, no connections. My bigger problem was that I knew nothing about the principles of my new career. I needed to meet people, present my services, make requests, stand up for my rights—and I couldn''t. It all boiled down to one reason: shyness.

We have two brains. Our old brain only knows fight or flight. Our new brain is trainable. In today''s world, instead of fleeing, the old brain feels shyness; instead of attacking, it shows aggression. The first root of shyness is that we don''t have the right files in our brain to show us how to behave courageously. The second root lies in a corner of your brain that connects experiences to emotions—past painful experiences make that corner afraid of social situations.',
'If you had all the courage in the world right now, what would you do?');

-- Chapter 2
INSERT INTO public.reading_sections (content_id, sort_order, heading, body, quote) VALUES
('c2b3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f12345678901', 2, 'The Downsides of Timidity',
'The more you become aware of the harms of timidity, the more inclined you''ll be to become courageous. Let me teach you the Triple Opinion Technique first.

When you have a different opinion and want to voice it, use this formula: Fact #1 – Fact #2 – Opinion. State two facts everyone would agree with, then give your personal viewpoint. This way, you come across as thoughtful and persuasive rather than impulsive.

Now let''s look at financial harms. Has someone borrowed something and not returned it? Have relatives used your help without paying? Were you unable to ask for the salary you expected? Have you invested in a one-sided relationship? Did shyness stop you from asking about opportunities others were seizing?

Have you paid more than your fair share? Yielded too easily in negotiations, buying things you didn''t want? Signed a bad deal because you couldn''t say no? Kept your skills hidden while someone else presented that same idea and reaped the benefits?

You need six communication "files": Saying No, Claiming Your Rights, Receiving Criticism, Dropping Excessive Politeness, Expressing Opinions, and Making Effective Requests. By constantly reminding that corner of your brain how much timidity has hurt you, you gradually realize that being courageous doesn''t hurt—being timid does.',
'Shyness causes all kinds of damage—many types even worse than financial loss.');

-- Chapter 3
INSERT INTO public.reading_sections (content_id, sort_order, heading, body, quote) VALUES
('c2b3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f12345678901', 3, 'Cultivating Courageous Habits',
'Look at two women side by side. The courageous one holds her head high, smiles, looks straight ahead, shoulders relaxed back, spine straight. The timid one has her head down, chin tucked, shoulders slumped, arms crossed, feet turned inward.

Our inner state affects our body posture—but even more importantly, our body posture affects our inner state. Researchers proved this with the $50 Gamble Experiment: people who held power poses for a few minutes were far more willing to take risks than those in timid poses. Even people who were usually cautious became risk-takers after power posing.

Two body language habits you must develop: Eye Contact and Smiling. When someone speaks with you, meet their gaze—it shows the person is important to you. And wear a smile, even in professional discussions. A smile signals composure and engagement, and it feeds back into your inner state, making you feel more empowered.

What if these aren''t habits yet? Practice in safe situations. Use reminders. Gradually challenge yourself to sustain eye contact and a friendly expression even under stress. You might struggle initially, but with consistent practice, these behaviors will become natural parts of your Courageous Character.',
'Your body language absolutely affects your inner feelings—strike a power pose before any important meeting.');

-- Chapter 4
INSERT INTO public.reading_sections (content_id, sort_order, heading, body, quote) VALUES
('c2b3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f12345678901', 4, 'Healthy Assertiveness',
'Self-expression is the pearl in the shell of your confidence. The ability to voice what''s in your heart—that''s the simplest definition of self-expression. More formally, it means asserting your rights and articulating your thoughts, feelings, and beliefs in an effective, direct, and honest way.

Some people speak their mind but in a way that attacks others—that''s aggressive self-expression. Others never express what''s inside at all—that''s passive self-expression. What we aim for is assertive behavior: expressing yourself while achieving your desired outcome without trampling on others'' rights.

The ideal behavior of a courageous woman has three characteristics. First, your self-expression should be assertive—you voice your wants, feelings, and beliefs openly. Second, your self-expression should be respectful—you regard both yourself and the other person as worthy of respect. A courageous Ladyboss believes that all human beings are worthy of respect.

Even if someone makes a serious mistake, there are proper channels to address wrongdoing. But you, as a courageous woman, never allow yourself to insult someone. The goal is to express yourself clearly, honestly, and respectfully—achieving what you need while maintaining dignity for everyone involved.',
'A courageous Ladyboss believes that all human beings are worthy of respect.');
